Mild acute rhinosinusitis presenting with facial heaviness and non-purulent discharge
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Facial pressure worse leaning forward, Non-purulent rhinorrhea, Nasal mucosal congestion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-013
Reason for Visit: Worsening facial pain and pressure requiring assessment to rule out complicated acute bacterial rhinosinusitis or periorbital cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted escalating facial pain that worsened with movement, mimicking early orbital spread. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to palpate the sinuses, evaluate extraocular movements, and definitively rule out periorbital extension or neurologic involvement prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My face feels incredibly heavy and throbs whenever I bend down to tie my shoes.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports bilateral dull facial pressure localized over the maxillary and frontal sinuses, evolving over the past 3 days following mild viral coryza. Describes exacerbation of facial heaviness when bending forward. Notes clear to white non-purulent anterior rhinorrhea and a mild intermittent post-nasal drip. Mild nasal mucosal congestion is present. Denies high-grade fever, purulent thick discharge, or severe unilateral dental pain.
Functional Status: Fully independent, working from home.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discomfort is impairing concentration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 80 | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, speaking with a mildly hyponasal voice.
Physical Exam: Maxillary and frontal sinuses are mildly tender to direct palpation. Normal periorbital soft tissue without swelling or erythema. Extraocular movements are intact and painless. Diagnostic triad confirmed: facial pressure worse leaning forward, clear non-purulent rhinorrhea, and nasal mucosal congestion visible on anterior rhinoscopy. Normal olfactory sensation. Oropharynx is clear.
Environmental Observations: Dust accumulation visible in the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent use of oral decongestants with short-lived relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild acute rhinosinusitis presenting with facial heaviness and non-purulent discharge - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symptoms present for only 3 days, lack of purulent/colored discharge, and absence of high fever or "double sickening."

Periorbital Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal periorbital soft tissues, no erythema, and intact painless extraocular movements.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral dull pressure rather than unilateral sharp, electric shock-like pain triggered by light touch.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with escalating facial pressure and headache that could signify a dangerous progression to acute bacterial sinusitis with potential orbital or intracranial complications. Delaying the physical examination until the next available in-hours appointment carried the risk of missing signs of periorbital cellulitis. An urgent home assessment allowed for safe confirmation of uncomplicated viral/inflammatory rhinosinusitis, ensuring immediate symptomatic management and avoiding premature antibiotic prescribing while confirming ocular safety.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommendation for regular intranasal corticosteroid spray (e.g., fluticasone) and saline nasal irrigations. Short-term (maximum 3 days) use of topical nasal decongestants if congestion is severe.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform saline nasal rinses twice daily leaning over the sink, linking the action directly to routine morning and evening tooth-brushing to ensure compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Saline sinus rinse kit.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if symptoms persist beyond 10 days or if high fever develops.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience swelling or redness around the eyes, double vision, a severe unremitting headache, or a stiff neck.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is consistent with a viral or early inflammatory process; secondary bacterial infection can develop later.
Clinical Course: Viral rhinosinusitis typically resolves entirely within 7 to 10 days with supportive care.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your nasal discharge becomes thick and bloody, if you develop a fever over 39C, or if the skin around your eyes becomes swollen.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sinus cavities are inflamed and swollen, trapping clear mucus. Saline rinses physically wash out the mucus, and the steroid spray reduces the swelling to allow normal drainage.
Actionable Steps: Use saline rinses twice daily. Take regular simple pain relief. Do not use over-the-counter decongestant sprays for more than 3 consecutive days to prevent rebound congestion.

